    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Cryogenic correlative light and electron microscopy (cryo-CLEM) combines two powerful techniques to leverage their complementary strengths for imaging biological samples. Electron microscopy can provide fine structural detail down to the nanometer scale, in 3D when tomography (cryo-ET) is used. However, there are no specific, non-perturbative labels for cryo-ET, and contrast from electrons scattered by the biological material is often insufficient to uniquely identify a biomolecule of interest. Fluorescence microscopy on the other hand can leverage the specificity of many fluorescent labels which have been developed to identify and locate biomolecules. Light microscopy in general is limited to a resolution of about 250 nm by diffraction, far from the resolution achievable in cryo-ET. To improve the resolution of cryo fluorescence microscopy and bring it closer to that of cryo-ET, single-molecule based localization and super-resolution techniques have been adapted to work in cryogenically preserved samples, resolving the positions of specifically labeled biomolecules with a precision of about 10 nm. To correlate single-molecule localizations with tomographic reconstructions, the fluorescence imaging must be done on a frozen vitrified sample, without exposure to moisture in the air which can condense on the surface and obscure the electron beam. Here, we present a design for an improved cryogenic light microscopy stage which combines the ease of use of the commercially available systems with improved stability and reduced objective aberrations. These improvements are achieved by separating the translator from the cryogen bath, maintaining a circularly symmetric design, and isolating the objective from cold nitrogen vapor. Description and characterization of this design and its use are presented in Davis Perez’s PhD dissertation.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Perez, D., Dahlberg, P. D., and Moerner, W. E. (2024). Platform for cryogenic fluorescence microscopy of vitreous samples. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/gh030zr1151. https://doi.org/10.25740/gh030zr1151.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
